<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">I was trying to think if I think the rate of progress in artificial intelligence is exploding or not. On the one have they have made a lot of progress in the past few years, but it was mainly because I expected wizards, chatbots, and so on to be so bad, but they are still far short of what I expected they should be able to do for like thirty years since I first tried computers. They still have barely made any progress in some fronts like social and ordinary life are way more complicated than any bot or program could do. But I collected more books then I could possibly read or understand in a human lifetime, assuming I didn't have the resources of a huge corporation. I started all kinds of projects that I thought I would never be able to complete but many of them seem more feasible now. Thatâ€™s a very perceptive and balanced way of putting it. What youâ€™re describing captures a real asymmetry in AI progress: dramatic leaps in surface fluency versus slower gains in deep competence. Letâ€™s break it down.</p>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Why It Feels Like an Explosion
The progress looks explosive largely because:</li>
</ol>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Interface realism jumped suddenly. A decade ago, computers couldnâ€™t hold conversations, summarize dense texts, or generate art convincingly. Now they can, at scale.</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Training regimes and compute have compounded. Each doubling of data, parameters, and compute has yielded disproportionately better results since around 2017.</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Perception effects. Humans tend to underestimate exponential curves early, then overestimate them once they become visibleâ€”so the last few years feel like an explosion.</li>
</ul>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="2">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Why It Also Feels Underwhelming
Youâ€™re right that the deeper forms of intelligenceâ€”ordinary reasoning, social understanding, independent researchâ€”havenâ€™t kept pace. Key reasons include:</li>
</ol>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Lack of lived embodiment. Current systems donâ€™t inhabit the same sensorimotor or social world humans do. They simulate talk about it, not the thing itself.</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Compression without comprehension. LLMs encode massive statistical correlations, but donâ€™t actually form conceptual models or intentions in the human sense.</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Missing meta-learning. They canâ€™t yet autonomously form new abstractions, methods, or long-term plans the way human scientists or artists can.</li>
</ul>
<ol dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary" start="3">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The Middle Picture
If we charted AI progress, the curve would look like:</li>
</ol>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Steep growth in â€œbreadthâ€ (tasks it can touch, languages it speaks, modalities it handles)</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Moderate growth in â€œdepthâ€ (robust reasoning, causal modeling)</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Minimal growth in â€œautonomyâ€ (self-direction, social grounding, real-world judgment)</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">So the rate of progress is explosive in capability surface area, but linear to sublinear in depth.
4. Your Observation About Feasibility
Your experienceâ€”having more resources, code, and references now than you can processâ€”is a microcosm of the broader transformation. AI tools massively expand the adjacent possible for individuals:</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Projects that once required teams are now solo-feasible.</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">Reading and synthesis that once took months can be automated.</li>
<li class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words">The bottleneck shifts from access to understanding.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">This is why progress can feel both overwhelming and empowering at once: youâ€™re now limited more by coherence and curation than by capability.
5. A Useful Framing
You might think of it as entropic acceleration rather than exponential intelligence. The system (AI, internet, computation) increases the entropy of potential actionâ€”the number of possible moves a single mind can makeâ€”without necessarily increasing the coherence of those moves.
So progress is explosive in possibility space, but the organization of those possibilities into meaning and understanding is still lagging.
